} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488406 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488420 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\sin x}{1x}$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "347a250b-fde2-504e-aba8-c4e6cad7f81f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488420 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}\\cdot (x)-(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (x)^{\\prime}}{(x)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (x)-(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (1)}{(x)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(\\pi)&=\\frac{\\pi\\cos(\\pi)-\\sin(\\pi)}{1\\pi^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{\\pi(-1)-0}{1\\pi^{2}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{\\pi}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- \\frac{1}{\\pi}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488422 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488433 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "827bc541